## Start where the information is richest

Moth should simplify a program while its types, values and lifetimes are still
known, then generate straightforward WebAssembly. The execution environment
can turn that Wasm into efficient machine code for its CPU. This division avoids
building a second general-purpose native optimiser inside Moth.

This document owns optimisation goals, pass placement and the policy shared by
Moth's Wasm backend and Lappet. The compiler and memory references still own
language semantics, validation and physical ownership. The opportunities below
are design priorities, not claims that the corresponding passes exist.

For example, Moth knows that a generic operation has been instantiated with a
particular numeric type. It also knows which error channel is declared and
whether a returned value may retain an input allocation. Those facts can remove
work before Wasm is emitted. A native compiler receives the resulting functions
and memory operations, not Moth's complete type or lifetime model.

## Keep three decisions separate

The builder selects a Moth compilation profile, a target capability contract and
a native compilation strategy. These selections have different purposes.

The Moth profile controls optional analysis effort and permitted physical
representation. Target validation checks that the selected executable can be
implemented. The native strategy controls how Wasm is translated to machine
code. None changes exact numeric types, required access validation or observable
error behaviour.

Lappet V1 uses Wasmtime with Cranelift only. Development defaults to
`OptLevel::None` to favour native compilation time and release defaults
to `OptLevel::Speed` for execution performance, subject to benchmarks.
Moth's output profile remains independent of the native optimisation
level. Lappet configures the engine without post-emission Wasm passes.

Keep Cranelift's default backtracking allocator and no function inlining
until independently justified. Wasmtime 49.0.3 describes its faster
single-pass allocator as not production-ready. Winch is deferred until
compatibility, stability and measured benefits justify another backend.
Exact proposals, debugger support and runtime policy are checked at
Lappet's own engine milestone.

## Preserve required compiler work

Some transformations are part of compiling Moth correctly, even when they also
make the output smaller. Constant evaluation where required, concrete generic
completion, ordinary static branch selection and result-channel construction
follow their existing compiler contracts. A debug profile cannot disable them
as though they were optional optimisation passes.

Both branches of ordinary static control flow receive the required frontend
validation before executable selection. Reachability does not make an authored
source dependency valid after the fact. Compiler-owned link facts determine
which already validated functions and runtime requirements enter an assembly.

Borrow and lifetime-topology validation also remain mandatory. Failing to prove
an optional transfer can retain storage conservatively. Failing to prove legal
lifetime topology cannot be repaired by a collector or by native optimisation.
A capable full-control release backend keeps its collector-free guarantee.

## Use Moth's semantic evidence first

### Concrete types and binding instances

Complete generics before executable lowering. Keep owner-local generic
parameters and canonical concrete instances distinct. Reuse the established
specialisation and binding-instance owners instead of cloning a function or
creating a second type-instantiation table inside the backend.

Trusted scalar-parametric Core bindings resolve their exact semantic types
before selecting an implementation. A narrow numeric value carried in an i32
register remains narrow. A Dec value does not become F64 to fit an available
helper. Its result may share an operand representation under the declared
provenance contract, so selection is not automatically a fresh allocation.

File-backed WAT or JavaScript supplies implementation code, not permission to
redefine a Core operation. Reuse registered adapters and helper identities.
Ordinary foreign annotations never gain trusted memory access from their file
location or a matching function name. Unsupported representations retain their
capability diagnostics.

### Known values and checked arithmetic

Use existing constant and numeric evidence before adding another analysis.
The current numeric-proof contract supports a bounded set of integer-check
elisions. It does not establish arbitrary range analysis, loop bounds or
floating-point reassociation. Broader proofs need an explicit owner and tests.

A successful proof can remove a runtime predicate while retaining the original
operation, evaluation order and declared source contract. For example, proving
that one concrete call cannot overflow does not erase a public Error channel
or make an otherwise required handler optional.

Exact integer domains, finite binary floats, per-operation precision and Dec
rounding remain language rules. Native wrapping arithmetic is not an implicit
substitute for a checked Moth operation. Fast-math or other constraints use
their separately approved semantics rather than inheriting whatever an engine's
aggressive setting happens to permit.

### Allocation, retention and copies

Moth owns the information needed to reason about allocation families, aliases,
retained edges and cleanup frontiers. Keep those decisions in their existing
analysis and memory-planning owners. A Wasm optimiser is not expected to recover
them from pointer arithmetic or calls to runtime helpers.

The compiler's validated memory plan is the sole final authority for layout,
physical strategy, cleanup and retained-edge transitions. It can express stack
or inline placement, affine cleanup, regions and selective retained-edge
counting. The lowerer realises that plan rather than choosing a cheaper-looking
strategy independently.

Prioritise avoiding unnecessary allocation and copying, shortening valid
retention and fusing redundant planned runtime transitions. Each change must
preserve explicit copy semantics, internal sharing, result provenance and all
exit paths. A same-family replacement must not become a temporary
last-reference decrement followed by an increment.

Physical refinement and coalescing happen before plan validation and
fingerprinting. Late code generation cannot split an escaped allocation or
invent a new cleanup owner. Read the memory authority for the complete proof
conditions rather than duplicating them in a new optimisation pass.

### Calls, results and templates

Preserve zero, one and multiple success slots and the separate error channel.
Avoid materialising a tuple or temporary record merely because a target ABI
packs results. Packing belongs at that ABI boundary. Evaluate each producer
once and read success results only on the success continuation.

For templates, keep folded strings folded and lower runtime construction through
the existing string operations. Investigate repeated allocation or copying at
that owner before adding a general string optimiser. Structural resource
strings still need the builder's validated output context before their concrete
characters can be observed.

## Generate Wasm that exposes useful work

Emit structured control flow, exact scalar operations and explicit stable calls
through the existing Wasm lowerer. Avoid dispatcher loops, redundant carrier
conversions and unnecessary memory round trips when ordinary lowering already
has the facts to omit them.

This is good lowering, not a reason to add a broad optimisation pipeline. Keep
register allocation, CPU instruction selection and target-specific code-quality
heuristics in the native backend. Native optimisers can also simplify scalar
expressions and control flow where the emitted representation exposes those
relationships.

Keep `wasm-encoder` as the binary writer. Structured Wasm LIR, explicit
target-feature validation and faithful encoding of validated physical
memory plans are the relevant improvements. Replace the existing dispatcher
loop through the accepted Wasm backend plan rather than adding a pass to
recover natural control flow.

The Wasm roadmap now owns three tooling slices: `wasmparser` validation with
target-specific features, real function/local names through
`wasm_encoder::NameSection` and optional `wasmprinter` inspection of
the emitted binary. The printer belongs to development/tests rather than
the runtime. Review aligned wasm-tools versions at that checkpoint.

A Rust host call can hide an implementation from the Wasm-to-machine-code
compiler. Prefer a direct Wasm instruction or existing trusted Wasm helper for
a small operation when it preserves the complete contract. Use Rust bindings
for host services and genuinely native libraries rather than routing every
Core operation through the host for uniformity.

Guest-to-host conversion is still a real cost. For game engines, measure small
frequent calls and useful batches. For servers, also measure buffer sizes,
allocation and time spent in native services. A change that improves a scalar
microbenchmark but increases boundary copying may be a regression overall.

## Place future passes deliberately

Start a new pass only after identifying work that existing owners cannot remove
more directly. Use this order when investigating a measured problem:

1. Correct inefficient lowering or duplicated runtime work using facts already
   available to its owner.
2. Exploit type, value, access or lifetime evidence before that information is
   erased.
3. Leave ordinary native code-quality work to Cranelift and measure the result.
4. Add a dedicated Wasm transformation only when it still provides a worthwhile
   benefit for supported consumers.

Inlining is a useful example. Moth-level inlining could expose semantic facts
that native compilation cannot see. It could also inflate Wasm, repeat analysis
and slow both compilation strategies. Native inlining capabilities and defaults
must be verified rather than assumed. Neither blanket Moth inlining nor blanket
delegation is the policy. Investigate selected call sites with a code-size and
compile-time budget.

Loop transformations, vectorisation, general bounds-check elimination
and post-emission tools follow the same rule. A future offline `wasm-opt`
experiment can compare binary size, optimisation time, cold Cranelift
compilation and runtime performance against direct structured emission.
Adopt no mandatory Binaryen pipeline without evidence beyond existing
lowering and native optimisation. Verify observable Moth semantics and
browser performance before broadening the policy.

## Preserve evidence across transformations

Published module results, generated sidecars, HIR and their proof tables are
immutable. A pass cannot rewrite an executable while retaining analyses that
were proved for its previous form.

A proposed transformation must name its input owner, output owner, valid phase
and affected proofs. If it changes control flow, aliases, lifetimes or physical
obligations, it must be placed before the corresponding validation or use an
explicitly reviewed revalidation handoff. Builders and Lappet do not mutate HIR,
run raw compiler stages or repair analysis tables themselves.

Recompute or invalidate affected interface, implementation, runtime and physical
fingerprints through their existing owners. An optimisation creates no parallel
semantic identity scheme. A cache hit or a native engine's successful validation
cannot establish Moth memory-model correctness.

## Measure the stages independently

Keep source compilation, Wasm generation, engine compilation, instantiation and
execution as separate measurements. Include output bytes, allocations and
resident memory where relevant. Host binding measurements should distinguish
dispatch, conversion and the host operation itself.

For a representative program, compare four configurations:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    Moth development output + Cranelift None
    Moth development output + Cranelift Speed
    Moth release output     + Cranelift None
    Moth release output     + Cranelift Speed
]

This separates Moth's optimisation and physical planning from native
optimisation. Measure code size and compilation time as well as steady-state
speed. `SpeedAndSize`, future Winch and browser runtimes are separate
experiments. Neither native setting proves performance on every CPU.

Lappet V1 has no persistent AOT cache. A cold compilation benchmark creates the
required fresh engine/module state and records any process or operating-system
reuse. Execution benchmarks compile outside the timed loop. In-process module
reuse is valid application behaviour but must not be labelled cold startup.
Record the engine version, Cargo features, host target, CPU, Moth profile,
strategy, limits, instrumentation and workload with the evidence.

Use matched repeated measurements under the repository benchmark policy. Keep
correctness tests separate from timing thresholds. A performance result does
not prove numerical, error or ownership equivalence.

## Gate each new optimisation

Before implementation, record the eliminated work, the semantic evidence that
permits it and the owner that supplies that evidence. Identify any downstream
pass that could already do the same work. Define the fallback when optional
proof is unavailable and the budget for code size, compilation and memory.

Tests must preserve exact types, evaluation order, observable errors, allocation
relationships and cleanup on every affected exit. Add negative cases where the
proof does not apply. Measure both game and server workloads and applicable
browser output before widening a local result into a general policy.

The Moth roadmap owns compiler work. Lappet's separate roadmap owns runtime
configuration and interoperability. Updating this authority coordinates those
owners without activating their paused work or authorising an unreviewed pass.

